Package: rmoriebricklayer
Title: Reproducible Data Capsules with Provenance and Fallback
Version: 1.0.0
Authors@R:
    person(c("Vansh", "Singh"), "Ruhela", email = "vsruhela@proton.me",
           role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0009-0004-1750-3592"))
Description: For analyzing tables that others publish, such as open-data
    portals and statistical agencies, so that a result can be traced to the
    exact data behind it and checked again later. A 'capsule' records where
    each source came from, its Secure Hash Algorithm 256 ('SHA-256') digest,
    the schema it should have and an archived copy in the Internet Archive
    'Wayback Machine' (<https://web.archive.org/>); sources are found through
    the Comprehensive Knowledge Archive Network ('CKAN', <https://ckan.org/>).
    Run again, a capsule says whether each source still matches and, if it
    does not, how its distribution moved (Kolmogorov-Smirnov, chi-square,
    population stability index and 'Benford' first-digit screens), and when a
    source cannot be reached it runs on schema-driven synthetic data that is
    marked as such. Capsules can be signed with keyed digests ('HMAC-SHA-256',
    RFC 2104) or with the post-quantum signature standards 'ML-DSA' (FIPS
    204), 'SLH-DSA' (FIPS 205) and 'XMSS' (RFC 8391), implemented from the
    standards with no system cryptography library and checked against
    'OpenSSL' 3.5 and the known-answer vectors of 'NIST' (the US National
    Institute of Standards and Technology). For the administrative tables
    these capsules usually hold, it computes period-over-period change with
    exact intervals, trends (Mann-Kendall with 'Theil-Sen' slopes), rates,
    and the bounds that rounding and suppression in a release imply; small-area
    rates with the empirical Bayes shrinkage of Clayton and 'Kaldor' (1987)
    <doi:10.2307/2532003>; self-exciting event series as 'Hawkes' processes;
    custodial populations by stock-and-flow measures; and the burden of air
    pollution from dispersion to attributable deaths. A compiled core is shared
    with sibling packages through 'LinkingTo'.
